(4969465, 282340, 96534, '<td valign="top">Hi, I have been shown this image because I''m in some doubt as to why I''m about to be disqualified for doing exactly what you did here - I increased the vignette on Lightroom under the ''lens correction'' tools. I agree with you wholeheartedly, this shouldn''t be seen as any more than basic editing - your shot is fantastic, as is the rest of your work, and you did what I would call a global change, which should be allowed under basic editing rules. Not only that, but its a lens correction - if you can crop, rotate, adjust levels, desaturate any colour you wish and thereby creating a completely different effect, I don''t see why you can''t increase the vignette. <br/>Congratulations on your amazing photos, they are all worthy of constant ribbons! <br/>cheers,<br/>Trish</td>', 'Hi, I have been shown this image because I''m in some doubt as to why I''m about to be disqualified for doing exactly what you did here - I increased the vignette on Lightroom under the ''lens correction'' tools. I agree with you wholeheartedly, this shouldn''t be seen as any more than basic editing - your shot is fantastic, as is the rest of your work, and you did what I would call a global change, which should be allowed under basic editing rules. Not only that, but its a lens correction - if you can crop, rotate, adjust levels, desaturate any colour you wish and thereby creating a completely different effect, I don''t see why you can''t increase the vignette. Congratulations on your amazing photos, they are all worthy of constant ribbons! cheers,Trish', '2008-09-23 01:46:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(6570701, 279728, 12611, '<td valign="top">Fantastic. The feel of this shot puts me in the spot; the road, the pavement, the trees, the wide-openess, one of my favorites for sure. I like the fact you are looking back at the camera, although it might work if you were walking away from the camera down the road.</td>', 'Fantastic. The feel of this shot puts me in the spot; the road, the pavement, the trees, the wide-openess, one of my favorites for sure. I like the fact you are looking back at the camera, although it might work if you were walking away from the camera down the road.', '2011-10-18 10:26:50.000000', NULL, 0);
